--TEST--
`Core\Cache::process()` names the process's one store rather than handing back a container, and that store is not the per-core one: a handle taken after every write reads them all, and a key written on one tier is not the key read on the other — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim `cache-local-twice-is-one-store-and-not-two.nvst` makes about the
// per-core tier, asked of the per-process one: the value `process()` answers is a
// name for the store and not the thing holding the entries, so a second handle
// reads what the first wrote.
var $first = Core\Cache::process();

// Taken before a single entry exists, so nothing it reads below can be a
// snapshot it was handed at this line.
var $second = Core\Cache::process();

array<string> $keys = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"];
$first->put("alpha", "<alpha>");
$second->put("beta", "<beta>");
$first->put("gamma", "<gamma>");

// A third handle, taken after all three writes, reads every one of them —
// counted rather than read off a line, because a store that were per-handle would
// answer for the two its own handle wrote and `null` for the third, which is a
// plausible-looking half.
var $third = Core\Cache::process();
string $back = "";
int $seen = 0;
foreach ($keys as string $key) {
    $back = $third->get($key) as string;
    if ($back == "<" . $key . ">") {
        $seen = $seen + 1;
    }
}
echo "a handle taken last reads them all: ", $seen, " of ", Core\Arr::count($keys), "\n";

// And the two in-process tiers are two stores rather than one under two names,
// which is what a member each buys: what a program wrote where is visible in its
// source instead of in an argument.
var $core = Core\Cache::local();
$core->put("alpha", "<the core's own>");
echo "the process tier keeps its own entry: ", $third->get("alpha") as string, "\n";
echo "and the per-core tier keeps its own: ", $core->get("alpha") as string, "\n";
echo "a name only the process tier has: ", $core->get("beta") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
--EXPECT--
a handle taken last reads them all: 3 of 3
the process tier keeps its own entry: <alpha>
and the per-core tier keeps its own: <the core's own>
a name only the process tier has: null
